From 669f3b22db0686b53852812b4b7e587ede093102 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 06:09:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add a delay-probe report - the tool that actually picks the
 evaluation delay

The delay probe (14e0f0b) writes raw observations to logs/delay-probe.jsonl
but nothing reads them yet. This is that reader: it answers the roadmap's
top open item directly - at 30s/120s/300s after detection, what fraction of
tokens would pass minUniqueWallets/minTransactionCount/maxTopHolderPercent
today, and how do the underlying numbers move with age.

src/analysis/delayProbeAnalysis.ts
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pure aggregation, no fs/config/network, matching src/analysis/paperPerformance.ts's
split so it stays testable without loadConfig() dragging RPC_URL validation
into an offline analysis tool (CLAUDE.md: "src/analysis/ is deliberately
config-free"). Groups by delaySeconds and computes, per bucket:

* dropped vs errored, told apart and both excluded from stats - a dropped
  observation (queue full, never attempted) and an errored one (fetch threw)
  are different failure modes and neither has real numbers to average in.
* per-metric min/median/p90/max, with null values counted (nullCount) and
  excluded from sampleSize rather than treated as zero.
* passRate: of observations where all three filtered metrics are non-null
  ("evaluable"), the fraction that pass at the given thresholds. Reports
  null (not 0%) when nothing is evaluable - 0% would misleadingly imply a
  real failing measurement rather than an absence of one.

scripts/analyze-delay-probe.ts
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLI wrapper: npm run report:delay-probe. Reads logs/delay-probe.jsonl (three-way
missing/empty/unparseable handling, same as analyze-paper-performance.ts's
readJsonl), prints a console summary, writes a markdown report under reports/.
Thresholds default to config/default.json's shipped values but are overridable
by flag so a re-tune doesn't silently change what an old report means.

tests/test-delay-probe-report.ts (31 assertions, offline)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Covers: coverage lines never counted as observations, dropped/errored
distinction, median/p90 on a known distribution, nulls never averaged as
zero, pass-rate boundary conditions (exact threshold, missing-field
exclusion, null-not-zero when nothing is evaluable), multi-bucket sorting,
distinct-mint counting, and malformed delaySeconds excluded rather than
grouped under NaN.

Verified: npm run typecheck clean, npm test all offline suites still green
(482 offline assertions total now, up from 451), new suite run standalone
with zero env vars set to confirm it is genuinely config-free. Manually
exercised the CLI end-to-end against a synthetic 123-line fixture (40 mints
x 3 delays + a drop + an error + a coverage line) - report rendered a
sensible pass-rate curve (0% at 30s, 20% at 120s, 75% at 300s on the
synthetic data) and wrote to reports/ correctly. Fixture and its output were
local-only and not committed (both gitignored).

Does not touch any PASS/SKIP/trading decision - read-only reporting on a
log file, same as the paper-performance analyzer. Wired into tests/run-all.ts
and package.json (report:delay-probe, test:delay-probe-report).

Next real step needs your Mac: run the bot in paper mode for a while
(delayProbe.enabled is already true by default) to generate real
logs/delay-probe.jsonl data, then `npm run report:delay-probe` on it to
actually pick the delay from real numbers instead of the synthetic ones
used to verify this tool.

